{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/913405-so-old-so-little-xp-an-in-universe-explanation","id":913405,"title":"So old, so little XP: an in universe explanation.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-06-13T08:41:07-07:00","id":913405,"title":"So old, so little XP: an in universe explanation.","content":"When one compares character of different life span there is a problem that inevitably shows up. Why are the long lived character not leagues beyond everyone else in term of power. <br /> Consider vampire, your average Ancilla has 15 xp beyond a bog standard starting character. That is a ridiculously tiny amount for anyone who has been alive for anywhere between 50 and 150 years.<br /> <br /> Characters who don't live nearly as long as vampire would accumulate the same amount of XP in a couple years. So why is it that vampire gain XP so slowly? Are they unable to change? Not at all. Not in second edition.<br /> <br /> No, the reason vampire gain so little XP is blood.<br /> <br /> The blood is the life, the blood is everything. Or at least Vitae is. <br /> Vitae is the single most important thing in any vampire's life. It allows them to function, it replaces almost every mortal needs, it provides more pleasure than any other activity. I propose that everything vampire do is actually geared toward acquiring vitae.<br /> All the politics over territory are about acquiring vitae, all the powers they develop are about being predator to acquire vitae, all the political struggles are about being in a position where you can circumvent the feeding restriction.<br /> <br /> Of course vampire aren't monomaniacal, they do other things than pursue Vitae, but any such pursuit is either secondary to Vitae or serve, even tangentialy, as a way to acquire vitae.<br /> <br /> So how does it explain the slow trickle of XP vampire have? Consider blood potency. The higher blood potency rise the deeper your reserves of Vitae become. Simply put, the older a vampire get the less often he actually needs to feed. A neonate has very low reserve, any exertion of power, every waking up at night drain their reserves deeply comparatively. This induce a sort of hyperactivity into neonates. Not only that but they still function upon a mortal point of view. <br /> <br /> An elder sees time differently, he can slow down, take the long view. but more importantly he doesn't have to feed as often, he doesn't expand the same amount of his vitae reserves through living than neonates do. <br /> A neonate expand 10% of his reserves just to wake up. To an elder this might be as little 1.3%. Neonates have few means to acquire said Vitae asside from direct hunting. Elders have acquired power structures that make such hunting unecessery.<br /> <br /> Essentialy, the older a vampire becomes the less he actually has to do. They have less drive, less need. Until they drop into torpor, lose their power base and reserves of vitae and suddenly the cycle of activity picks up once more when they wake.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2547-elderand","name":"Elderand","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"98"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-13T09:25:24-07:00","id":913444,"title":"","content":"NIce write up. <br /> <br /> If i may, i would also add that often times you can achieve elder age by playing it safe and avoiding the risk that would pan out xp. (both in a mechanical sense and in-universe view). In the same vein a popular tactics is working through proxy. So the elder get the benefit, but not the xp.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Completed campaign: Scion 2nd Edition. Les L\u00e9gendes Currently playing: Being a dad for a 3 year old daughter and a 2 years old son and now a beautiful new baby.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11312-maitrecorbo","name":"Maitrecorbo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11312_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"719"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-14T18:05:04-07:00","id":914509,"title":"","content":"Both interesting ideas but the first has a fatal flaw: Every Elder had to start off as a Fledgling and go through that period of hyper activity before settling down in time. This should translate to more XP than the pittance reflected at character creation.<br /> <br /> I had assumed the reasoning was similar to the second. By playing it safe and keeping ones head down, thereby not getting into life-threatening situations all the time, one could reach old age with less risk but also fewer major unlife lessons experienced and internalized. Ones time would still be filled with <i>something</i>, just not as much desperate or harrowing something as another, more brash approach.<br /> <br /> I would imagine that many or most elders would fall into this category simply because of the inevitably higher rate of attrition suffered by those whose more ambitious requiems were cut short due to one reckless mistake.<br /> It's kind of like the old FBI saying(if you replace the po-po with.. Death) &quot;He has to be lucky <i>every</i> time, but I only have to be lucky <i>once</i>.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11661,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11661-rathamus","user":"Rathamus","date":". 06-14-2016, 09:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11661-rathamus","name":"Rathamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11661_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-15T04:52:26-07:00","id":914749,"title":"","content":"I think it is important to consider those statements in the character generation chapter in the context they are presented in, that is to say the (player) character generation chapter. I do not think those starting EXP values are meant to actually be used to create comparative NPCs. They are there so you can create advanced PCs that can grow into their role without quickly exceeding their supposed NPC peers. Imagine if a PC ancillae started with roughly the same amount of EXP a proper NPC ancillae should have, soon enough they would exceed the top-dog elders in the domain in power, simply because of the rapid growth that being a PC allows you.<br /> <br /> However, even taking this into account, I agree with the <i>general </i>idea of this thread. Often times when statblocks of elder NPCs are presented they seem a bit lacking for creatures that had centuries to learn without physical or mental degradation.<br /> <br /> As for your idea of the &quot;sluggish elder&quot;, I do not think this is actually the direction they intend to go in 2e. Going by the outline for A Thousand Years of Night, the average elder is meant to be much more active, canny and powerful than that (although I personally like the sluggish elder as a motif, but rather as an exception than the rule).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-15T05:07:44-07:00","id":914756,"title":"","content":"The laying low explanation makes sense to an extend. It's okay for fledglings who are still pretty much humans with a sun allergy and a hunger for blood, but soon enough, when your mortal friends die or it just becomes impossible to rely on them without them finding out about you, you just have to get up and get stuff done. Playing it safe is actually way harder than it sounds when you're an undead monster. It means you at least develop enough skills to pass unnoticed. You gotta make sure other vampires don't consider you a big enough rival that they'll get up in your face for feeding territory. That means making sure they like you enough or making sure your feeding is as discreet as possible. That's either social skills or stealth which have to be pretty high if you want to keep that up for a long time. Investigation is good too if you want to clean after you and not leave traces of your actions. I mean, even the tranqul vampire who doesn't want to mess with anyone and tries to feed on animals is going to get pushed around sooner or later. If they're weak, they're a potential minion, if they're strong, they're in the way. I'd argue that staying out of the way other vampires enough that you're never really threatened takes just as much effort as it takes to actively participate in the politics and fighting.<br /> <br /> Actually, I just completely ignore the book's take on how much xp characters should have and give my NPCs more XP. Better yet, I don't think in terms of XP, just &quot;what stats and abilities do I want this NPC to have to be a suitable challenge for the PCs&quot;.<br /> <br /> To be frank, I don't even use the XP system in the book. It's kind of well thought, but I rarely use conditions except when they're inevitble like for disciplines (and I don't use the beats that come with them), I really don't feel like keeping track of fifth of xp points and would probably forget to give them half the time. I just give out an amount of XP that I decide at the beginning of some sessions, and I'm probably a bit more generous with them than the book says (cause both me and the players like that their charaters grow relatively fast, especially since our game only lasts about a year and a half), except maybe if I gave out like ten conditions per session and they were all resolved and stuff, but no way I'm micromanaging that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7104-s-brechen","name":"S. Brechen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"171"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}